misc: hy310-motor: homing at probe is on again, as in stock

Patch 0156 turned it off on 12.09.2026 because only the lower edge of the range gate had
been observed and homing drives up to 100 msteps upwards first. On 23.09.2026 the upper edge
was measured as well (the gate opens 90 msteps above a position inside the travel, closes
10 msteps back), and the same day showed what a boot has to be able to repair: the focus
stood 110 msteps above its travel, nothing was latched, both tools refused, and homing at
probe walked it back - "back in range after 110 DN msteps". That is the way back stock has
and a user without a shell needs. homing=0 keeps the bench behaviour.
---
--- a/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c
@@ -891,20 +891,27 @@
  * With homing=0 the driver probes, configures the pads and exposes sysfs
  * without touching the mechanism. It then has no calibration, and says so.
  *
- * The default is off, and that is a decision about shipped devices rather than
- * about benches: the module is =m and the node is enabled, so udev loads it at
- * every boot. Homing drives up to HOMING_UP_MAX msteps upwards first, and only
- * the LOWER limiter edge has ever been observed on this board (measured
- * 12.09.2026, analyse/boot/motor-bereichswaechter-20260912.txt). If the watcher
- * stays silent in that direction the driver never reverses and the run ends in
- * the mechanical stop -- once per boot. A focus needs no calibration to be
- * useful; step_cur is a counter and not a position anyway, since counter and
- * mechanism drift apart at every edge event.
+ * The default is on, as it is in stock, since 23.09.2026. It was off from
+ * 12.09. because only the LOWER edge of the gate had been observed then
+ * (analyse/boot/motor-bereichswaechter-20260912.txt) and homing drives up to
+ * HOMING_UP_MAX msteps upwards first: a watcher silent in that direction would
+ * have let every boot end in the mechanical stop. On 23.09. the upper edge was
+ * measured too - the gate opens 90 msteps above a position inside the travel
+ * and closes again 10 msteps back - and the same day showed what the boot has
+ * to be able to repair: the mechanism stood 110 msteps above its travel after
+ * a boot, no edge was latched, and both tools refused to move it. Homing at
+ * probe walked it back ("back in range after 110 DN msteps"). A stale pad
+ * level was the way it got there (see the pinctrl group for PH14 in the DT),
+ * and homing is the way back that stock has and a user without a shell needs.
+ *
+ * homing=0 keeps the old behaviour for a bench that must not move anything:
+ * the driver probes, configures the pads and exposes sysfs without touching
+ * the mechanism, and says that it has no calibration.
  */
-static bool homing;
+static bool homing = true;
 module_param(homing, bool, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(homing,
-		 "run the homing sequence at probe (default no, it drives into an unverified upper end)");
+		 "run the homing sequence at probe (default yes, as stock does)");
 
 /*
  * Pad-Bias des Endschalter-Eingangs.
